Actually better: use Blender's Python with numpy for image processing, since Blender ships numpy. But I also want to write derived PNGs. Blender can save images via bpy.data.images.new + pixels + save_render. That works.

Plan for textures:
- `Model/Generated/Textures/FigureSkin.png`: copy of figure-v5 (readable import settings applied to a COPY in Model/**, per instruction "set only your COPIES").
- We project the painting onto body/hands via UV mapping from figure texture space to mesh. That is: give each body vertex a UV equal to its projected position in the figure texture (planar front projection). Then the gold-skin painted texture shows on the body, preserving the painted look. For hidden/new surfaces (back side), the UV would sample wrong regions → need "coherent goldskin material": create a second material using a small patch of the figure's skin texture (e.g., a clean gold-skin region) tiled — that's the "coherent goldskin material" for hidden surfaces. Good: assign front-facing polygons (normal.z<0 i.e., toward camera) the projected material; back-facing/outward polygons the goldskin tiled material. Hmm, actually planar projection from front: front vertices UV = projected; back surfaces sample mirrored/other region. Using a goldskin patch material for back faces is what's requested.

This is a solid, honest approach.

For face and hair: "Face and flowing hair can be carefully projected shaped meshes as original premiums". So build a shaped head mesh (a real head-ish ellipsoid with nose/chin volume) with front projection UV onto the face region, plus hair as a shaped flowing shell (layered ribbons following the painted hair silhouette) with projected hair texture and alpha.

