Alternatively, the builder can just reference the FBX clip in both Animator states. Simpler and fewer failure modes: create two clip assets by copying. I'll implement copying (gives named Intro.anim / Loop.anim assets as the contract wants and avoids ImportFBX clip-name coupling). Actually CopyClip is easy:

```csharp
static AnimationClip Copy(AnimationClip src, string name){
  var c = new AnimationClip(); c.name = name; c.frameRate = src.frameRate;
  foreach(var b in AnimationUtility.GetCurveBindings(src)){ AnimationUtility.SetCurve(c, b, AnimationUtility.GetEditorCurve(src, b)); }
  foreach(var b in AnimationUtility.GetObjectReferenceCurveBindings(src)){ AnimationUtility.SetObjectReferenceCurve(c,b, AnimationUtility.GetObjectReferenceCurve(src,b)); }
  var s = AnimationUtility.GetAnimationClipSettings(src); AnimationUtility.SetAnimationClipSettings(c,s);
  return c;
}
```
Need src.legacy=false and read/write enabled → `AnimationUtility.GetCurveBindings` works on imported clips.

