Why doesn't the mask cut it? Because `on(i,j)` uses `mask[py,px]` — the mask is a 240x240 downsample of a boolean over the FULL image. When I pass rect_px and compute u,v from the rect, then sample mask at `u*(mw-1)`, `v*(mh-1)` — but the mask array covers the WHOLE image, not the rect! The u,v are normalized within the rect, so sampling the full-image mask at (u,v) samples the image's top-left region, not the rect.

Bug! I need to map (u,v) within the rect to image-normalized coordinates:
```
gx = (x0 + (x1-x0)*u)/FIG_W
gy = (y0 + (y1-y0)*v)/FIG_H
```
then sample mask at gx,gy.

That explains everything: the mask sampled a mostly-"solid" region in the top-left (the raised arm area) → the shell is nearly a full rectangle.

Same for build_face_shell (mask offset) — the face shell was masked to whatever the top-left region of the face mask was, producing a rectangle-ish plate. Indeed the "grey slab" is likely the face shell too... but the isolate_Body shows no slab, so the slab is entirely from the Hair mesh ✓ (the face shell is part of Body and looks fine).

Let me fix `on()` to use image-normalized coordinates.

